\f0\b\fs32 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Course Development for JOUR 3318: Digital Investigative Techniques
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 The rapid and unchecked acceleration of generative artificial intelligence is creating a crisis within the global information ecosystem. The digital public square is increasingly saturated with misinformation, sophisticated algorithmic manipulation, and automated disinformation campaigns. In this environment, the traditional models of journalism and media education are insufficient. Students need to become critical, adaptable creators capable of ethically and rigorously interrogating complex digital landscapes.
\f1 \

\f2 The development of the course JOUR 3318, \'93Digital Investigative Techniques,\'94 represents a vital and timely curricular intervention designed to address this challenge. As a foundational component of the newly approved nine-hour \'93Certificate in Writing and Researching with AI\'94 and the revamped Journalism and Digital Media minor, this course seeks to equip students with the advanced technical and analytical competencies required to combat digital manipulation. 
\f1 \

\f2 The primary goal is to empower students to create digital media that actively serves unmet public information needs, thereby fulfilling a core democratic purpose of journalism.
\f1 \

\f2 In the digital revolution, students are immersed in algorithmic social media feeds where they operate primarily as passive consumers of high-emotion, ideologically driven content. The instructional design of JOUR 3318 explicitly acknowledges this context, aiming to induce a cognitive recalibration that moves students away from passive consumption and toward a stance of active journalistic skepticism. 
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Stage 1: Identify Desired Results
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b Established Goals and Contextual Imperatives
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 The overarching objective of the course-design process is to equip students with critical competencies to combat digital misinformation and disinformation, empowering them to create digital media that actively serves public information needs. This objective directly supports the institution's strategic plan calling for courageous teaching and transformative learning that tackles pressing global problems.
\f1 \

\f2 However, the pursuit of these goals is complicated by sticking points inherent to the modern media landscape. One sticking point is the speed of the modern news cycle, which exerts immense commercial and psychological pressure on creators to publish content before all facts are thoroughly vetted. During breaking news events, the digital environment is flooded with user-generated content, much of which is unverified, without context (context collapse), or deliberately fabricated. Another sticking point involves the reliance on human memory and eyewitness accounts, which are inherently fallible and subject to bias. While digital evidence offers a potential remedy, the ease of deception and manipulation require the adoption of highly specialized technical countermeasures.
\f1 \

\f2 Consequently, the established goals for JOUR 3318 are defined by both philosophical realignment and technical mastery. The curriculum is anchored in the \'93discipline of verification,\'94 establishing this methodology as the fundamental differentiator between professional journalism and other forms of mass communication. Students must master specialized tools for vetting information and content, specifically targeting photographs, videos, and organizational statements to identify false and fabricated material. The course establishes the necessity of computational journalism, introducing students to why the Python programming language is highly useful for automating research, scraping web data, and bypassing the limitations of manual search queries. Furthermore, students must develop secure protocols for protecting information and archiving transient webpages and social media data before bad actors can scrub the digital record. Finally, a central goal involves critically evaluating the opportunities, challenges, and strict limitations of generative AI tools for deep research, ensuring students view these systems as professional competencies requiring oversight rather than mere automation shortcuts.
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \'a0
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b \'a0
\f1\b0 \

\f0\b Enduring Understandings
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 The first enduring understanding centers on the fundamental purpose of journalism and public information. Students will understand that journalism exists primarily to provide citizens with the verified, contextualized information necessary to make informed decisions about their lives, their communities, and the broader democratic society. When vital public information needs go unmet, potentially dangerous voids are created. Students will understand that these voids can be exploited by malicious actors, conspiracy theorists, and automated bot networks seeking to hijack public discourse, thereby eroding civic trust and destabilizing democratic institutions.
\f1 \

\f2 A second enduring understanding involves the complex relationship between honesty, objectivity, and cognitive bias. The course challenges abstract notions of perfect, bias-free objectivity, teaching students that such a standard is an impossible metric for human beings. Instead, the enduring understanding emphasizes that honesty and rigorous methodology matter profoundly. Students will understand that professional journalists must cultivate self-awareness regarding their own ideological and cognitive biases. Rather than pretending these biases do not exist, they must counter them by asking more questions of more people from vastly different perspectives, maintaining an internal inquiry regarding whether they are being honest with their audience.
\f1 \

\f2 The third enduring understanding addresses the architecture and intentions of information disorder. Students must understand that colloquial terms like fake news are analytically useless and actively harmful. Instead, they will understand the nuances of digital deception, categorizing problematic content based on the intersection of factual falsity and the explicit intent to cause harm. This involves understanding the precise differences between misinformation (unintentional errors), disinformation (fabricated content designed to deceive), and mal-information (genuine information weaponized out of context). 
\f1 \

\f2 The final enduring understanding requires a philosophical interrogation of communication, language, and meaning-making in the era of artificial intelligence. Drawing upon classical philosophy and critical media studies, students will understand storytelling as essential to the human experience. It helps us convey meaning, express emotion, and better understand the human condition. Conversely, they will understand that Large Language Models (LLMs) operate as statistical prediction engines devoid of semantic understanding, truth, or connection to objective reality. By internalizing Harry Frankfurt\'92s philosophical distinction between lying (which acknowledges the truth by attempting to conceal it) and bullshit (which is entirely indifferent to the truth), students will understand that the uncritical deployment of generative AI can function as an automated engine of the latter, representing a profound threat to the concept of truth.
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 Essential Questions
\f1\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf2 The pedagogical design for JOUR 3318 is driven by the following essential questions:
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 \cf2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Does the truth matter in a heavily mediated, algorithmically driven society, and how can verified reality compete with emotionally driven digital persuasion? 
\f1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}What are the fundamental intentions, financial incentives, and political goals behind the creation and amplification of mis/disinformation? 
\f1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}What vital public information needs go unmet within our communities, and how does the creation and sharing of fabricated media exploit these voids? 
\f1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Is deliberate lying, or the frictionless proliferation of automated bullshit that ignores objective reality entirely, a greater threat to democratic discourse? 
\f1 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}How can journalists and researchers utilize generative artificial intelligence to enhance their educational and professional enrichment without outsourcing their ethical responsibility for meaning making? 
\f1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

